From 2a234f4444dea7c0ad8947f0678fa642d13b080d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 17:20:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Remembered rows: tell a removed owner from an
 offline one, and use bulk publication lookups only

The first commit of this PR failed four existing tests on Linux, each pinning a real rule:
- test_kanban_remote_moved_owner "named owner offline: its silence proves nothing": an owner with a stale info row
  and no records is offline, not removed, and its copy must not be retired.
- test_kanban_replica_bystander_memory (three): the remembered-card path may not scan an all-row aggregate, a dead
  scope's publication keys are not looked up, and a malformed created_at must not raise.

Changes:
- "Removed" now means: no `gateway/info` row (`known_gateways`, read on every call and never memoized, so an
  unrelated gateway's row rebuilds nothing), no task record (`publishing`, the projection's per-gateway seen set)
  and no fresh scope heartbeat. Evidence without `known_gateways` counts nothing as removed. This also tightens
  the `owner_removed` release of e9c07f61d27 the same way.
- No walk of the owner set: `_release_removed_owners` takes only the moved evidence. "Nobody publishes the card"
  for the aged-out case and "another gateway publishes it" for the copy case both use the publications
  `remembered_publications` resolves in one scan; that now also covers rows of unlisted gateways.
- A row with no usable created_at proves nothing in either case.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_release_removed_owner_rows.py adds the offline-versus-removed distinction on
the pure rule and the malformed created_at case; the replica fixtures give cards a created_at. The four existing
tests pass unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
